WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.570
Welcome to our Parents and Carers
workshop, supporting your Autistic

2
00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:09.640
child to flourish.

3
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:11.900
I'm glad you've made some time for this.

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.760
You might be arriving with a clear
question, or after a very full

5
00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:15.380
morning.

6
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.740
Either way, you're welcome here. Today

7
00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.520
we'll build on what you already know about
your child,

8
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:24.860
explore some shared language, and consider
supports that fit your family.

9
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.600
Keep your workbook nearby.

10
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.600
In this walkthrough, activity pauses are
brief.

11
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.860
Whenever you need more time to think,
write or talk,

12
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.500
pause the video and resume when you're
ready.

13
00:00:38.841 --> 00:00:43.763
I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians
of the lands on which we are learning

14
00:00:43.764 --> 00:00:48.878
today, and pay my respects to Elders past
and present. I recognise the continuing

15
00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:53.418
connections of Aboriginal and Torres
Strait Islander peoples to Country,

16
00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:58.213
culture and community. I extend that
respect to Aboriginal and Torres Strait

17
00:00:58.214 --> 00:01:01.409
Islander people taking part in this
learning today.

18
00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.500
Our Statement of Reconciliation is in your
workbook for you to read.

19
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.640
For the sake of this demonstration, I'll
read it aloud.

20
00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.600
We at Positive Partnerships value and
recognise the diversity of

21
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.040
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
cultures and heritage

22
00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.240
and their proud part in Australia’s
national identity.

23
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.250
We acknowledge and continue to learn about
Australia’s colonial

24
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:31.360
past and the historic and ongoing impacts
on Australia’s First Nations people.

25
00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.990
We believe in and strongly support the
Truth-telling of

26
00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:37.860
Australia’s colonial history

27
00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.570
and in moving forward as a nation we must
encourage open,

28
00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:46.000
honest dialogue and build relationships
built on respect and trust.

29
00:01:49.401 --> 00:01:53.793
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

30
00:01:53.794 --> 00:01:56.319
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

31
00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.600
Everyone is welcome to be themselves here.

32
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.920
You can move, stand, fidget, listen,
write,

33
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.400
step out or pass on an activity.

34
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:07.120
Use whatever helps you take part.

35
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.560
Some parents and carers here may be
Autistic

36
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.760
or otherwise neurodivergent themselves.

37
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.240
Your experience is welcome too.

38
00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:16.680
Your workbook belongs to you,

39
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.600
and you decide what to share.

40
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.120
Please keep other families' stories
private.

41
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.680
When we discuss school or family
situations,

42
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.200
leave out identifying details.

43
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:29.280
We can learn from a situation while
respecting the people involved.

44
00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:34.430
Before we settle in, take a moment to
check your surroundings: the

45
00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.640
exits, bathroom facilities, refreshments
and any quiet space available.

46
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.240
Follow the arrangements for your venue,
including smoking and vaping.

47
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.560
If you're watching elsewhere, make the
space as comfortable as you can.

48
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.440
Keep your phone available if you need to
be contacted by school or a

49
00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.600
carer, and be considerate about sounds and
calls.

50
00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.520
Have your workbook and something to write
with nearby.

51
00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:01.420
You can write questions down, and in a
live workshop you can ask the

52
00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:03.560
delivery team privately or during a break.

53
00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:06.740
Attend to your own needs as we go.

54
00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.900
Let's meet the people around us. At your
table, share your first name

55
00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.600
and who you're here for.

56
00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.240
You might include your child's age or year
level, if you'd like to.

57
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.000
Then share one thing you would like to
understand by the end of the day.

58
00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.040
Keep it brief so everyone has room, and
remember you can pass.

59
00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.470
There's no need to share a diagnosis,
school name or private family

60
00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:33.280
circumstances.

61
00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.880
If you're watching on your own, make a
note of your question on page four

62
00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:37.920
of the workbook.

63
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:39.520
Pause here for your introductions.

64
00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:51.520
Let's come back. Write your one thing on
page four if you haven't

65
00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:52.040
already.

66
00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:56.600
Keep that question with you. We'll return
to it in our final session

67
00:03:56.600 --> 00:04:00.830
and consider what has become clearer, as
well as what you'd still like

68
00:04:00.830 --> 00:04:01.480
to explore.

69
00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:05.880
Here's the shape of our day.

70
00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.960
We begin with your child as a whole
person, then explore sensory

71
00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.880
experience and thinking.

72
00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:16.080
After morning tea, we move into
communication, connection and daily

73
00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:16.880
capacity.

74
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.720
The afternoon turns towards home and
community, followed by partnership

75
00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:22.880
with school.

76
00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.880
Our final session brings the learning back
to a few small next steps.

77
00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.890
The timetable on the slide is a guide for
a live workshop, including

78
00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:38.880
morning tea from eleven to eleven fifteen
and lunch from twelve fifteen to twelve
forty-five.

79
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:42.880
In this recording, take breaks when you
need them.

80
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.830
These six sessions are connected, so keep
the same child in mind

81
00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:47.880
throughout.

82
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.240
A quick word about language.

83
00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:57.240
You'll hear us say Autistic child or
Autistic person.

84
00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:02.480
Many Autistic people prefer this
identity-first language because autism

85
00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:04.040
is part of who they are.

86
00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:08.400
Some prefer person on the autism spectrum,
or another description.

87
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.100
Each person's preference matters.

88
00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:15.520
Follow your child's lead wherever
possible, and be open to their

89
00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.280
preferences changing.

90
00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:21.800
You may still be finding words that feel
comfortable in your family.

91
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.620
There's no test on terminology today.

92
00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:28.040
Our purpose is to speak respectfully and
to listen to how people

93
00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:29.480
describe themselves.

94
00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.140
Bring your child to mind.

95
00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:36.420
What are three things you love or value
about them?

96
00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:40.240
It might be their humour, an interest they
know so much about,

97
00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.880
the way they connect with someone, or a
small moment of joy.

98
00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.440
Write those things on page four.

99
00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.690
For this moment, put the list of
challenges to one side and notice who

100
00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:51.180
they are.

101
00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:56.380
If this is a hard season, one small moment
from the past week is enough.

102
00:05:56.380 --> 00:06:00.220
Start privately, then share one thing with
your table if you wish.

103
00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.080
Pause now and give yourself time.

104
00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:12.360
Keep that page handy.

105
00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.520
Those strengths and joys belong in every
conversation we have today,

106
00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:17.880
including conversations about support.

107
00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.560
We'll come back to them at the end,

108
00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:21.960
when you choose a strength to name to your
child.

109
00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:26.320
You know your child in ways that nobody
else does.

110
00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:30.400
You know the familiar routines, the tiny
changes, and what a good

111
00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.240
moment looks like in your family.

112
00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:35.260
That knowledge matters here.

113
00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:39.720
We bring information about
neurodiversity-affirming practice to put

114
00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.720
alongside your experience.

115
00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:45.260
As we talk it through, you can ask what
fits, what doesn't, and what

116
00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:47.020
you still want to understand.

117
00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:51.280
You don't have to leave with someone
else's version of family life.

118
00:06:51.880 --> 00:06:55.620
We are looking for ideas you can consider
with your own child.

119
00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.680
We're going to hear directly from Autistic
young people.

120
00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.160
Listen to their accounts on their own
terms.

121
00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:06.200
Each speaker offers their own experience.

122
00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:08.080
We don't need to compare them with your
child

123
00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:11.400
or ask one person to represent everyone.

124
00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:12.600
Notice what stays with you,

125
00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.840
and what it makes you curious to
understand more deeply.

126
00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:16.680
Let's listen.

127
00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.360
Take a quiet moment with what you've just
heard.

128
00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:51.200
On page four, return to the young people's
voices.

129
00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:52.600
What did you notice?

130
00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:55.940
What stayed with you? And what would you
like to understand more deeply

131
00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:57.480
about your own child?

132
00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:59.680
Begin with a little private writing.

133
00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:02.920
If you share, stay close to what the
speakers actually said.

134
00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:06.910
It's fine to feel moved, uncertain, or to
have a question without an

135
00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:07.600
answer.

136
00:10:07.600 --> 00:10:11.480
You can talk with someone nearby or keep
this reflection to yourself.

137
00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:13.120
Pause the video now.

138
00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:24.320
As we come back, keep one question in
mind.

139
00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:27.280
Sensory experience, thinking and
communication

140
00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.280
will give us ways to explore it.

141
00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:31.840
A question that stays open can be useful.

142
00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:34.160
We can listen carefully without assuming

143
00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:37.360
we already know what an experience means
for someone else.

144
00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:44.360
Neurodiversity means the diversity of
human brains and minds.

145
00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:45.920
It includes everyone.

146
00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:48.760
Neurotypical and neurodivergent describe
patterns

147
00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.360
within that wider picture,

148
00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:53.000
and autism is one expression of it.

149
00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.920
These words help us talk about differences

150
00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.000
in experience and processing.

151
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.680
They don't tell us everything about a
particular person.

152
00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:03.440
As we explore common patterns today,

153
00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:06.560
keep asking how they do, or don't,

154
00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:08.880
connect with the child you know.

155
00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:10.880
Each child remains an individual.

156
00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:16.430
A neurodiversity-affirming approach
recognises different ways of

157
00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:18.480
knowing, doing, being and

158
00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:19.920
belonging.

159
00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:23.040
People think and learn in different ways.

160
00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:27.080
Everyone has strengths, and everyone can
experience challenges.

161
00:11:27.080 --> 00:11:29.680
Take the things you wrote about your child
earlier.

162
00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.740
Their interests, enjoyment and way of
connecting are part of the picture

163
00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:33.640
when we think about

164
00:11:33.640 --> 00:11:35.120
support.

165
00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:39.320
We can take difficulties seriously while
valuing who they are.

166
00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:42.510
That means asking what the surroundings,
routines and support need to

167
00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:43.600
look like so your child

168
00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:46.520
can participate comfortably and
authentically.

169
00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:49.700
A hard day doesn't cancel out a strength,
and recognising a strength

170
00:11:49.700 --> 00:11:50.360
doesn't make a

171
00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:51.560
hard day disappear.

172
00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:57.160
Autism is one part of a person's identity.

173
00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:00.240
Culture, language, gender, age,
disability, faith

174
00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.000
and family circumstances can overlap and
shape opportunities,

175
00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:05.340
barriers and experiences.

176
00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:08.080
That's the idea of intersectionality.

177
00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:13.160
The words on this slide are prompts to
widen our understanding.

178
00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:16.640
You don't need to share personal identity
information here.

179
00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:18.640
For your child, useful support takes
account

180
00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.720
of the whole person and the family
context.

181
00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:25.170
We can stay curious about parts of their
experience we haven't yet

182
00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:25.720
understood.

183
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.680
Capacity can change, sometimes within the
same day.

184
00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:34.960
Something your child managed on a quiet
Saturday

185
00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:37.920
might be much harder after a full Monday
at school.

186
00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:41.600
The child is the same, but the demands,
sensory load

187
00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:44.520
and available energy may be different.

188
00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:47.240
The phrase dynamic disability helps us

189
00:12:47.240 --> 00:12:49.480
keep that changing experience in view.

190
00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:52.160
When something becomes difficult,

191
00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:56.960
ask what else is going on and what support
fits now.

192
00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:59.720
Yesterday's capacity doesn't tell us

193
00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:02.120
exactly what is available today.

194
00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:03.640
We'll come back to this in more detail

195
00:13:03.640 --> 00:13:05.280
when we talk about daily energy.

196
00:13:07.600 --> 00:13:10.160
Let's hold on to joy and flourishing as we
move forward.

197
00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:13.960
Connection, belonging and small moments of
enjoyment matter.

198
00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:15.720
Every family is different, and
understanding

199
00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:17.640
what helps can take time.

200
00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:20.120
Doing something differently is fine

201
00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:21.360
when it helps your family.

202
00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:24.960
A quieter outing, a familiar shared
activity

203
00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:27.400
or a small success can matter just as much

204
00:13:27.400 --> 00:13:28.860
as a bigger milestone.

205
00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:32.480
In our next session, we'll begin with

206
00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:34.440
how the world arrives through the senses,

207
00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:36.960
and then explore attention and thinking.

208
00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:39.680
Keep your child's strengths alongside your
questions.

209
00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.640
Welcome to Session Two.

210
00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:51.720
Keep in mind the child you wrote about
earlier.

211
00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:54.200
We're going to build a fuller picture of
their experience,

212
00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:57.200
beginning with sensory processing, then
thinking

213
00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.200
and internal signals.

214
00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:02.920
Each idea is something to explore
alongside what you know.

215
00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:06.120
You may recognise some patterns and not
others.

216
00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:07.640
By the end, you'll have some questions

217
00:14:07.640 --> 00:14:10.160
to take into everyday routines at home.

218
00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:15.480
Before thinking, communication or
connection, there is sensory

219
00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:16.680
experience.

220
00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:19.680
The world is already arriving through our
senses.

221
00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:22.680
How it arrives can affect how comfortable
we feel,

222
00:14:22.680 --> 00:14:26.680
what we notice and how much energy is
available for everything else.

223
00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:27.680
Let's start there.

224
00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:32.120
What does sensory processing mean to you?

225
00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:33.840
Take a moment to put it into your own
words.

226
00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:35.640
You might think about an ordinary
experience,

227
00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:37.960
such as walking into a noisy room

228
00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:39.480
or putting on a warm jumper.

229
00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:42.880
Pause here if you'd like time to talk it
through.

230
00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:56.450
Sensory processing is the way the brain
receives, processes and responds

231
00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:57.200
to information from

232
00:14:57.200 --> 00:14:58.860
the senses.

233
00:14:58.860 --> 00:15:02.390
We often notice the response first, but
there is a whole process

234
00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:04.200
happening before that response

235
00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:05.160
becomes visible.

236
00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:11.860
Many of us learnt five senses: touch,
vision, taste, hearing and smell.

237
00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:14.400
Here we include three more.

238
00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:18.880
Proprioception is our sense of the body in
space, drawing on

239
00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:21.300
information from muscles and joints.

240
00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:24.560
It helps us know where our body is and how
much force we're using.

241
00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:28.880
The vestibular sense relates to balance
and movement.

242
00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:33.610
Interoception is our sense of internal
body signals, such as hunger,

243
00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:35.920
thirst or needing the toilet.

244
00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:37.880
We'll return to that later.

245
00:15:37.880 --> 00:15:40.140
These senses work together.

246
00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:44.010
In a busy routine, someone may be managing
several kinds of sensory

247
00:15:44.010 --> 00:15:45.300
information at once,

248
00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.000
including input that others barely notice.

249
00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.640
Think about taking off a jumper because
you're getting hot.

250
00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:56.640
First, information arrives from the body.

251
00:15:56.640 --> 00:16:01.020
The brain processes it, including what
similar experiences have felt

252
00:16:01.020 --> 00:16:01.640
like before.

253
00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:05.640
Then there is a response, such as taking
the jumper off.

254
00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:08.640
This process helps us understand our
surroundings,

255
00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:11.640
keep track of our body and engage with
what's happening.

256
00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:15.640
People process sensory information
differently.

257
00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:18.640
A visible reaction is just one part of
that process.

258
00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:23.130
Before judging the reaction, it helps to
ask what information may have

259
00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:23.640
arrived,

260
00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.640
and how it might have felt.

261
00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:30.040
We're going to watch two short clips.

262
00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:33.000
The first gives a sense of sensory
overwhelm.

263
00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.200
The second makes space for sensory joy.

264
00:16:36.200 --> 00:16:39.920
Both belong in our understanding of
Autistic experience.

265
00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:41.880
If the sound or images feel too much,

266
00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:45.600
you're welcome to lower the volume, look
away or pause.

267
00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.440
Listen to the account without assuming

268
00:16:47.440 --> 00:16:50.960
every Autistic person experiences things
in the same way.

269
00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:19.680
Let's leave a little space before we move
on.

270
00:19:23.240 --> 00:19:27.080
Now let's hear about sensory joy. Notice
the pleasure and richness that

271
00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:28.840
sensory experience can bring,

272
00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:31.840
alongside the overwhelm we've just
considered.

273
00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:27.320
Sensory experience includes comfort and
enjoyment too.

274
00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:31.080
A sound, movement or texture can be deeply
satisfying.

275
00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:34.160
Keep both sides in the picture when you
think about your child:

276
00:20:34.160 --> 00:20:37.710
what asks a lot of their senses, and what
feels good or helps them

277
00:20:37.710 --> 00:20:38.160
settle.

278
00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:44.440
This framework from Winnie Dunn describes
four patterns of sensory

279
00:20:44.440 --> 00:20:45.200
response.

280
00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:49.560
Registration means some input may not be
picked up readily.

281
00:20:49.560 --> 00:20:54.920
Sensory seeking means actively looking for
more input, perhaps through

282
00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:56.520
movement, touch or sound.

283
00:20:56.520 --> 00:21:02.190
Sensory sensitivity means noticing input
strongly, even if the person

284
00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:03.520
doesn't act to change it.

285
00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:08.670
Sensory avoiding means actively reducing
input, such as covering ears or

286
00:21:08.670 --> 00:21:10.520
choosing a quieter place.

287
00:21:10.520 --> 00:21:14.640
A person may seek one kind of input and
avoid another.

288
00:21:14.640 --> 00:21:19.270
The pattern can also shift with the
setting and the load already being

289
00:21:19.270 --> 00:21:20.120
carried.

290
00:21:20.120 --> 00:21:24.360
Some families will have encountered a
sensory profile through an

291
00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:25.800
occupational therapist.

292
00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:30.080
Use this as a way to notice patterns and
ask questions.

293
00:21:30.080 --> 00:21:33.200
It doesn't give us a complete description
of a child.

294
00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:38.840
Stimming means repetitive movement or
sound,

295
00:21:38.840 --> 00:21:42.760
such as rocking, flapping, spinning,
humming or tapping.

296
00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:47.040
It can help a child settle, focus, cope or
express joy.

297
00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:49.160
You may recognise small versions in
yourself,

298
00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:50.760
such as jiggling your leg.

299
00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:55.040
Notice what stimming seems to do for your
child.

300
00:21:55.040 --> 00:21:56.240
Make room for it at home,

301
00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:58.840
and help others understand its purpose.

302
00:21:58.840 --> 00:21:59.960
If something causes harm,

303
00:21:59.960 --> 00:22:02.960
the question becomes how to meet the same
need more safely.

304
00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:04.440
Simply taking it away

305
00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:06.440
can remove something that was helping.

306
00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:09.520
Some children hold their stims back at
school.

307
00:22:09.520 --> 00:22:11.600
Home can offer room for authentic
expression

308
00:22:11.600 --> 00:22:13.000
without that extra effort.

309
00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:18.560
Turn to the sensory activity on page six.

310
00:22:18.560 --> 00:22:20.840
Choose one familiar home routine:

311
00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:23.960
getting ready for school, a meal or bath
time.

312
00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:26.400
What sensory demands are involved?

313
00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:28.920
Think about sound, light, touch, movement

314
00:22:28.920 --> 00:22:31.760
and the less visible body signals too.

315
00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:35.120
What small change could make it easier

316
00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:37.120
or offer more choice?

317
00:22:37.120 --> 00:22:40.120
And what would you like to find out from
your child?

318
00:22:40.120 --> 00:22:43.760
Write the routine, the demand and a
possible support.

319
00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:47.720
Talk through your ideas with a partner or
table if you wish.

320
00:22:47.720 --> 00:22:49.120
Pause here for the activity.

321
00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:01.120
Look back at your notes. Keep what you
observed separate from what you

322
00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:02.720
think it might mean.

323
00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:07.280
A loud bathroom or an uncomfortable
texture gives us a useful question

324
00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:08.360
to explore.

325
00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:11.620
Your child's own communication will help
us understand whether the

326
00:23:11.620 --> 00:23:13.760
change actually helps.

327
00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:16.280
Start with one workable adjustment to the
routine.

328
00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:22.330
Now we move from how the world arrives to
how the mind takes it in and

329
00:23:22.330 --> 00:23:25.120
where attention goes.

330
00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:29.600
The next ideas describe patterns some
Autistic people recognise.

331
00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:32.770
Keep checking them against the person you
know, rather than assuming

332
00:23:32.770 --> 00:23:34.000
every pattern applies.

333
00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:40.520
The detail-first idea describes building
understanding from individual

334
00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:42.680
pieces towards a whole picture.

335
00:23:42.680 --> 00:23:47.380
Someone may notice a small change, an
inconsistency or a background

336
00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:50.440
detail before the overall situation
becomes clear.

337
00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:54.440
This can be connected with strengths such
as pattern recognition, deep

338
00:23:54.440 --> 00:23:59.040
memory, logical precision, honesty and
close attention.

339
00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:03.440
Think about what your child knows in
detail, or what they notice that

340
00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:04.920
other people miss.

341
00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:07.840
These strengths are present in everyday
life.

342
00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:10.880
Taking in a lot of detail can also take
effort.

343
00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:15.370
A task may be hard to begin if the larger
picture or first step is

344
00:24:15.370 --> 00:24:16.320
unclear.

345
00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:20.640
The same way of processing can bring depth
and also create demands.

346
00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:25.070
We can notice both without turning this
into a checklist that every

347
00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:26.320
child must fit.

348
00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:29.800
Think about your own attention for a
moment.

349
00:24:29.800 --> 00:24:31.640
Does it move between several things,

350
00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:34.480
or become deeply absorbed in one?

351
00:24:34.480 --> 00:24:36.880
The torch and floodlight image gives us

352
00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:38.720
two ways to picture that.

353
00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:41.960
Monotropism describes attention going
deeply

354
00:24:41.960 --> 00:24:44.040
into fewer things at a time.

355
00:24:44.040 --> 00:24:45.960
Autistic people help develop this way

356
00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:47.600
of understanding attention.

357
00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:51.800
Deep interests can offer learning, memory
and joy.

358
00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:54.120
When your child is absorbed in building,

359
00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:57.280
reading about space or listening to a
favourite song,

360
00:24:57.280 --> 00:24:59.920
their attention may be deeply invested.

361
00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:03.840
Asking them to stop for dinner can involve
real effort.

362
00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:06.400
Warning, time and a helpful transition

363
00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:08.000
can make a difference.

364
00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:11.160
That absorbed state, sometimes called
flow,

365
00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:13.000
is also something worth protecting.

366
00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:19.310
Executive functioning is a name for
processes we use to organise and

367
00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:21.160
carry out everyday tasks.

368
00:25:21.160 --> 00:25:25.350
The slide includes planning, organisation,
time management and working

369
00:25:25.350 --> 00:25:25.720
memory.

370
00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:30.600
It also includes thinking about our
thinking, managing emotions,

371
00:25:30.600 --> 00:25:31.640
flexibility,

372
00:25:31.640 --> 00:25:35.520
attention, getting started and persisting.
We use different

373
00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:38.280
combinations of these throughout the day.

374
00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:44.380
When a task is hard, identifying the
particular demand can help us find

375
00:25:44.380 --> 00:25:44.920
useful support.

376
00:25:46.040 --> 00:25:49.170
Let's look at getting started and
switching focus, because they ask

377
00:25:49.170 --> 00:25:51.320
different things of a person.

378
00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:56.160
Getting ready for school can sound like
one instruction, but it

379
00:25:56.160 --> 00:25:58.320
contains many steps.

380
00:25:58.320 --> 00:26:01.240
Where do I start? What comes next?

381
00:26:01.240 --> 00:26:03.800
What needs to be ready at the end?

382
00:26:03.800 --> 00:26:07.480
If the whole task isn't clear, the first
step may be difficult to find.

383
00:26:07.480 --> 00:26:11.040
A visible sequence or showing the first
step can help make the task

384
00:26:11.040 --> 00:26:12.480
more understandable.

385
00:26:12.480 --> 00:26:17.200
Before adding another reminder, ask which
part needs to become clearer.

386
00:26:18.760 --> 00:26:20.960
Switching focus is a different demand.

387
00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:24.400
A child deeply absorbed in play may need
time to disengage

388
00:26:24.400 --> 00:26:27.200
before they can take up the next activity.

389
00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:29.760
An unexpected interruption can feel much
bigger

390
00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:32.520
than it looks from the outside.

391
00:26:32.520 --> 00:26:36.840
Think back to the torch beam. A warning
that means what it says,

392
00:26:36.840 --> 00:26:41.080
time to finish a small part, and a clear
sense of what comes next

393
00:26:41.080 --> 00:26:43.640
can support a transition.

394
00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:46.800
Check what works for your child.

395
00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:52.170
This slide is reserved for a young
person's account of deep focus and

396
00:26:52.170 --> 00:26:53.400
transitions.

397
00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:58.070
The supplied workshop materials still show
a video placeholder here, so

398
00:26:58.070 --> 00:26:58.480
there isn't a

399
00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:00.880
film to play in this walkthrough.

400
00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:04.280
We'll move into the reflection using the
ideas we've just explored,

401
00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:05.360
while keeping that space

402
00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:07.760
for a first-person account when the clip
is available.

403
00:27:09.920 --> 00:27:13.700
Turn to page seven. What are your child's
deep interests? What do you

404
00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:15.520
notice when they're fully engaged?

405
00:27:15.520 --> 00:27:18.520
When is it difficult to get started or
shift attention?

406
00:27:18.520 --> 00:27:21.720
Think of a time they were completely
absorbed at home. What were they

407
00:27:21.720 --> 00:27:22.020
doing?

408
00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:25.770
Choose the questions that feel useful.
Begin with a real moment, then

409
00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:27.920
talk with your table or write privately.

410
00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:31.720
Keep enjoyment and strengths alongside the
difficult transitions.

411
00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:33.720
Pause now for your discussion.

412
00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:46.400
As you return, notice the difference
between getting started, staying

413
00:27:46.400 --> 00:27:47.400
absorbed and switching

414
00:27:47.400 --> 00:27:48.400
away.

415
00:27:48.400 --> 00:27:51.480
Each may call for a different kind of
support.

416
00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:54.700
Naming what you see gives you something
specific to explore with your

417
00:27:54.700 --> 00:27:54.960
child.

418
00:27:54.960 --> 00:27:58.320
Keep the interest itself in the picture as
a source of enjoyment.

419
00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:03.680
Let's return to interoception, the body's
inside sense that we named

420
00:28:03.680 --> 00:28:04.040
earlier.

421
00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:07.240
Internal signals add another part to the
picture.

422
00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:12.330
Some experiences are hard to notice or
describe even when they strongly

423
00:28:12.330 --> 00:28:14.020
affect how a person feels.

424
00:28:16.760 --> 00:28:20.790
Interoception concerns signals from inside
the body: temperature,

425
00:28:20.790 --> 00:28:22.980
hunger, thirst or needing the bathroom.

426
00:28:22.980 --> 00:28:28.340
Alexithymia describes difficulty
identifying and describing emotions

427
00:28:28.340 --> 00:28:29.600
and feelings.

428
00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:34.500
They're related ideas, but noticing a body
signal and knowing how to

429
00:28:34.500 --> 00:28:36.780
describe an emotion are different tasks.

430
00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:41.740
A child might notice hunger only when it
becomes intense, or find it

431
00:28:41.740 --> 00:28:46.010
hard to tell whether a fluttery feeling
before a party is excitement or

432
00:28:46.010 --> 00:28:46.660
worry.

433
00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:49.640
These examples give us questions to
explore,

434
00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:54.000
rather than a diagnosis. We can offer time
and ways to communicate what

435
00:28:54.000 --> 00:28:58.140
is happening inside, without insisting on
an immediate answer.

436
00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:04.430
Sometimes a build-up is happening inside
even when the surface doesn't

437
00:29:04.430 --> 00:29:04.800
show it.

438
00:29:04.800 --> 00:29:09.800
Early signs may be subtle, individual or
simply unavailable to us.

439
00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:13.760
This is an invitation to learn together,
never a reason to blame

440
00:29:13.760 --> 00:29:15.760
yourself for not noticing sooner.

441
00:29:15.760 --> 00:29:19.600
Over time, your child and the people who
know them may build a shared

442
00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:21.160
picture of what helps.

443
00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:25.880
Describing changes clearly can help family
members and school respond

444
00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:27.040
more consistently.

445
00:29:27.820 --> 00:29:30.770
The picture should stay open to what your
child communicates and to

446
00:29:30.770 --> 00:29:32.500
things we haven't understood yet.

447
00:29:35.600 --> 00:29:39.160
Stay on page seven for Reading the signs.
What changes do you notice before

448
00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:40.640
your child becomes overwhelmed?

449
00:29:40.640 --> 00:29:44.440
Describe what you can see or hear, as
specifically as you can.

450
00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:48.800
What would be useful for another carer or
school to know?

451
00:29:48.800 --> 00:29:51.720
You might also write what you aren't sure
about yet.

452
00:29:51.720 --> 00:29:55.360
Take a little private thinking time, then
share only if you wish.

453
00:29:55.360 --> 00:29:57.880
Pause the video while you make those
notes.

454
00:30:06.480 --> 00:30:09.760
Keep that description available for our
work on school partnership.

455
00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:13.880
A useful note helps someone recognise a
change and respond with support.

456
00:30:13.880 --> 00:30:17.670
It doesn't need to explain everything, and
it can change as you learn

457
00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:18.080
more.

458
00:30:18.080 --> 00:30:21.880
We've now considered sensory experience,
thinking and internal signals

459
00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:23.200
as connected parts of

460
00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:24.320
the same person's day.

461
00:30:27.000 --> 00:30:28.840
That's our second session.

462
00:30:28.840 --> 00:30:32.080
After morning tea, we'll continue with
communication, connection and

463
00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:34.040
managing daily capacity.

464
00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:36.360
Take a break now if you need one.

465
00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:39.880
When you return, bring the same child to
mind, along with the strengths

466
00:30:39.880 --> 00:30:41.720
and questions you've been gathering.

467
00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:49.400
Welcome back.

468
00:30:49.400 --> 00:30:53.400
This session continues the picture we've
been building.

469
00:30:53.400 --> 00:30:56.400
Sensory experience, thinking and internal
signals can all shape

470
00:30:56.400 --> 00:30:58.960
communication, connection and

471
00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:01.600
the energy available across a day.

472
00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:04.800
Keep your own child in mind as we explore
these together.

473
00:31:07.160 --> 00:31:10.040
Let's begin with communication.

474
00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:12.520
Before thinking about anyone else's way of
communicating,

475
00:31:12.520 --> 00:31:14.040
we'll notice how many different ways

476
00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:15.600
we already use ourselves.

477
00:31:18.240 --> 00:31:21.520
Since you woke up today, how have you
communicated?

478
00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:23.440
Think of three different ways.

479
00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:26.360
It could be a spoken greeting, a message,
a wave,

480
00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:28.280
a look, pointing to something,

481
00:31:28.280 --> 00:31:30.840
or taking a moment before responding.

482
00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:32.280
Have a quiet think,

483
00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:35.120
then share one example if you'd like to.

484
00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:36.360
Pause here for a moment.

485
00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:48.120
We all move between different ways of
communicating.

486
00:31:48.120 --> 00:31:50.240
Speech is one part of that picture.

487
00:31:50.240 --> 00:31:56.030
Writing, gesture, a device, showing a
photo or moving away can carry a

488
00:31:56.030 --> 00:31:57.520
message too.

489
00:31:57.520 --> 00:32:00.450
Keep that variety in mind when we consider
what helps someone

490
00:32:00.450 --> 00:32:03.140
understand and express themselves.

491
00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:08.520
The two sides of this slide separate
understanding, information coming

492
00:32:08.520 --> 00:32:10.040
in, from expressing,

493
00:32:10.040 --> 00:32:13.400
messages going out. How much someone
speaks doesn't tell us how much

494
00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:15.120
they understand.

495
00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:19.250
A person may speak fluently and still need
support to understand spoken

496
00:32:19.250 --> 00:32:20.280
information.

497
00:32:20.280 --> 00:32:24.680
Someone else may understand more than they
can readily express.

498
00:32:24.680 --> 00:32:28.840
A A C means augmentative and alternative
communication.

499
00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:33.090
It can include signs, a communication
board or book, typing or a

500
00:32:33.090 --> 00:32:34.920
communication device.

501
00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:39.080
Some people use it alongside speech, and
some instead of speech.

502
00:32:39.080 --> 00:32:42.540
Communication can change with the setting,
the demands and how someone

503
00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:43.480
is feeling.

504
00:32:43.480 --> 00:32:47.320
Repeated words or familiar scripts can
carry meaning too.

505
00:32:47.320 --> 00:32:49.800
Stay curious about their purpose and the
context.

506
00:32:51.520 --> 00:32:54.280
As you watch this video, notice what
supports communication.

507
00:32:54.280 --> 00:32:56.600
Choose one idea that stands out:

508
00:32:56.600 --> 00:32:59.280
perhaps a communication method, something
another person does,

509
00:32:59.280 --> 00:33:01.320
or something about the environment.

510
00:33:01.320 --> 00:33:03.080
Let's hear the speakers' own accounts.

511
00:38:37.560 --> 00:38:41.160
What stood out to you about what supported
communication?

512
00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:43.320
Take a moment to think, or share one
observation

513
00:38:43.320 --> 00:38:44.640
with someone nearby.

514
00:38:44.640 --> 00:38:47.040
Stay close to what the speakers said.

515
00:38:47.040 --> 00:38:48.520
Pause here if you'd like longer.

516
00:38:56.600 --> 00:38:59.000
Keep that observation with you.

517
00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:02.520
There is no single Autistic communication
profile.

518
00:39:02.520 --> 00:39:05.080
We'll now consider the people and places
in your child's own

519
00:39:05.080 --> 00:39:06.120
communication world.

520
00:39:09.120 --> 00:39:12.720
Turn to the communication network on page
nine.

521
00:39:12.720 --> 00:39:16.290
Your child is in the centre. Around them
are home, school, friends,

522
00:39:16.290 --> 00:39:18.240
community and supports.

523
00:39:18.240 --> 00:39:20.520
Add one person or place now.

524
00:39:20.520 --> 00:39:23.520
You can write, draw or use a few words.

525
00:39:23.520 --> 00:39:25.960
There is no need to fill every box.

526
00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:29.960
A network can be small, changing or still
developing.

527
00:39:29.960 --> 00:39:33.760
A grandparent, teacher or coach may see
different parts of

528
00:39:33.760 --> 00:39:35.040
communication.

529
00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:37.440
Pause while you add one starting point.

530
00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:49.400
You can return to the rest later,
including with your child and other

531
00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:51.200
communication partners.

532
00:39:51.200 --> 00:39:56.170
This page helps us notice who is connected
and what they might need to

533
00:39:56.170 --> 00:39:56.640
understand.

534
00:39:56.640 --> 00:40:00.160
There is no ranking of who knows your
child best.

535
00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:05.200
On the same page, let's think about what
useful knowledge could travel

536
00:40:05.200 --> 00:40:06.680
between people and places.

537
00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:09.080
Purple is a communication strength.

538
00:40:09.080 --> 00:40:11.920
Blue is something that helps
communication.

539
00:40:11.920 --> 00:40:14.420
Orange is something you would like others
to know.

540
00:40:14.420 --> 00:40:18.420
Use coloured notes if you have them, or
the three workbook boxes.

541
00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:20.320
Write one, two or all three.

542
00:40:20.320 --> 00:40:24.380
You can use a made-up example, and you can
keep your notes private.

543
00:40:24.380 --> 00:40:28.560
If you share at a table or on a board,
leave out identifying details.

544
00:40:28.560 --> 00:40:31.196
Pause now for writing and optional
sharing.

545
00:40:39.200 --> 00:40:41.120
Look across those ideas.

546
00:40:41.120 --> 00:40:44.640
What strengths might be missed if we
looked only for speech?

547
00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:48.840
Which supports involve changing what a
communication partner does or

548
00:40:48.840 --> 00:40:50.200
provides?

549
00:40:50.200 --> 00:40:55.080
Keep a blue note, or something from the
What helps box, in mind.

550
00:40:55.080 --> 00:40:57.200
That's our link to the next slide.

551
00:40:57.200 --> 00:41:00.720
Useful information can help a child avoid
having to start again with

552
00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:01.680
every new person.

553
00:41:04.320 --> 00:41:07.560
Here are three options to consider
alongside what you've written.

554
00:41:07.560 --> 00:41:12.610
Clear communication means familiar,
specific words and one idea or

555
00:41:12.610 --> 00:41:14.520
step at a time.

556
00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:18.040
Processing time means allowing space to
understand,

557
00:41:18.040 --> 00:41:21.320
organise a response and communicate it.

558
00:41:21.320 --> 00:41:25.400
Visual supports mean showing information
as well as saying it.

559
00:41:25.400 --> 00:41:28.440
The useful support depends on the person
and the moment.

560
00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:32.200
Try leaving a pause without filling it
with more words.

561
00:41:32.200 --> 00:41:35.320
Keep the person's communication methods
available,

562
00:41:35.320 --> 00:41:37.840
including when the message is no.

563
00:41:37.840 --> 00:41:40.720
These are ways to make communication more
accessible,

564
00:41:40.720 --> 00:41:42.800
and we need to check whether they help.

565
00:41:44.560 --> 00:41:48.400
Visual supports are information you can
see and come back to.

566
00:41:48.400 --> 00:41:51.960
On this slide, a timer answers how much
time is left.

567
00:41:51.960 --> 00:41:54.760
A sequence answers what happens next.

568
00:41:54.760 --> 00:41:57.120
A daily schedule could answer what is
happening today,

569
00:41:57.120 --> 00:42:01.080
although that example space is blank in
the supplied slide.

570
00:42:01.080 --> 00:42:03.800
You might already use a shopping list,
calendar

571
00:42:03.800 --> 00:42:05.600
or reminder on your phone.

572
00:42:05.600 --> 00:42:07.640
What visual information do you use at
home,

573
00:42:07.640 --> 00:42:08.920
and what makes it helpful?

574
00:42:08.920 --> 00:42:11.840
Share a brief example if you wish,

575
00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:13.680
or note one privately.

576
00:42:13.700 --> 00:42:14.780
Pause here.

577
00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:26.200
The helpful format differs from person to
person.

578
00:42:26.200 --> 00:42:30.060
It might be an object, photo, symbol or
written words, on paper or a

579
00:42:30.060 --> 00:42:31.360
device.

580
00:42:31.360 --> 00:42:35.320
Check that the information makes sense and
is available when needed.

581
00:42:35.320 --> 00:42:38.920
A visual should offer information.
Communication tools still need to

582
00:42:38.920 --> 00:42:40.560
stay available in their own right.

583
00:42:42.760 --> 00:42:46.080
Connection involves understanding in both
directions.

584
00:42:46.080 --> 00:42:49.940
The double empathy idea, introduced by
Autistic researcher

585
00:42:49.940 --> 00:42:53.220
Damian Milton, asks us to consider
misunderstandings

586
00:42:53.220 --> 00:42:55.760
between people with different experiences

587
00:42:55.760 --> 00:42:59.220
and communication styles as a shared gap.

588
00:42:59.220 --> 00:43:01.180
The image shows two people sending

589
00:43:01.180 --> 00:43:03.380
and receiving in their own ways.

590
00:43:03.380 --> 00:43:06.060
Both have something to understand.

591
00:43:06.060 --> 00:43:07.460
You may already do this at home

592
00:43:07.460 --> 00:43:09.380
as you learn your child's communication

593
00:43:09.380 --> 00:43:11.260
and they learn yours.

594
00:43:11.260 --> 00:43:14.180
We can ask what we, as communication
partners,

595
00:43:14.180 --> 00:43:16.460
might change to make the exchange easier.

596
00:43:16.460 --> 00:43:20.220
Listening and adjustment belong on both
sides.

597
00:43:22.320 --> 00:43:26.440
Let's hear one person's experience of
being social.

598
00:43:26.440 --> 00:43:29.740
As you listen, notice what connection
means in this account, without

599
00:43:29.740 --> 00:43:31.000
expecting it to describe

600
00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:33.697
every Autistic person's experience.

601
00:44:20.760 --> 00:44:22.260
Take a quiet moment.

602
00:44:22.260 --> 00:44:23.620
What did you notice?

603
00:44:23.620 --> 00:44:26.480
You can share one reflection or hold it
privately.

604
00:44:26.480 --> 00:44:28.500
Pause the video if you need longer.

605
00:44:36.520 --> 00:44:39.720
Carry that reflection into the next
discussion.

606
00:44:39.720 --> 00:44:42.720
Connection can take different forms, and
the conditions around it

607
00:44:42.720 --> 00:44:44.520
can make a difference.

608
00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:49.670
Social connection can happen in family
life, school, the community and

609
00:44:49.670 --> 00:44:50.140
online.

610
00:44:50.140 --> 00:44:54.900
It may involve conversation, shared
interests, doing something

611
00:44:54.900 --> 00:44:58.170
alongside another person, quiet company or
contact that happens only

612
00:44:58.170 --> 00:44:59.220
occasionally.

613
00:44:59.220 --> 00:45:02.590
Sharing lots of detail about an interest
can itself be a way of

614
00:45:02.590 --> 00:45:04.340
reaching towards someone.

615
00:45:04.340 --> 00:45:08.920
Families and cultures also have different
ways of being together.

616
00:45:08.920 --> 00:45:12.380
Think about the connections your child
already enjoys.

617
00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:16.640
We can look for what feels meaningful and
workable for them, rather than

618
00:45:16.640 --> 00:45:19.860
using one style of interaction as the
measure for everyone.

619
00:45:23.040 --> 00:45:26.000
On page ten, think about when your child
seems most comfortable

620
00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:27.240
connecting.

621
00:45:27.240 --> 00:45:28.660
What do they enjoy:

622
00:45:28.660 --> 00:45:33.280
talking, gaming, sport, making something,
shared interests, or being

623
00:45:33.280 --> 00:45:34.960
together quietly?

624
00:45:34.960 --> 00:45:38.290
When does it feel enjoyable, and when does
it become tiring or

625
00:45:38.290 --> 00:45:39.520
overwhelming?

626
00:45:39.520 --> 00:45:42.550
Then consider what adults could change to
make connections safer,

627
00:45:42.550 --> 00:45:45.220
easier or more meaningful.

628
00:45:45.220 --> 00:45:46.940
Start with your child's preferences.

629
00:45:46.940 --> 00:45:50.280
You can reflect privately or talk with
your table.

630
00:45:50.280 --> 00:45:52.480
Pause for the discussion.

631
00:46:00.560 --> 00:46:03.960
As we return, keep one condition that
seems to help in mind.

632
00:46:03.960 --> 00:46:07.280
It could be the person, place, activity or
amount of time.

633
00:46:07.280 --> 00:46:10.830
Some children have few connections right
now, and this is not a

634
00:46:10.830 --> 00:46:12.160
comparison between families.

635
00:46:12.160 --> 00:46:15.810
Meaningful connection can look different
across people, settings and

636
00:46:15.810 --> 00:46:16.360
days.

637
00:46:18.440 --> 00:46:20.240
Now let's look at daily capacity.

638
00:46:20.240 --> 00:46:22.980
Something can be enjoyable and still take
energy.

639
00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:25.280
Communication, sensory processing,

640
00:46:25.280 --> 00:46:27.280
transitions and social connection

641
00:46:27.280 --> 00:46:30.180
all draw on the resources available in
that moment.

642
00:46:32.000 --> 00:46:35.160
Think of energy as a battery. Each day can
begin with a different

643
00:46:35.160 --> 00:46:37.700
amount of charge, and activities draw on
it.

644
00:46:37.700 --> 00:46:41.100
When it runs low, ordinary things can
become harder.

645
00:46:41.100 --> 00:46:45.360
Many people also use the spoons analogy: a
limited supply of energy,

646
00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:48.360
with different activities using different
amounts.

647
00:46:48.360 --> 00:46:53.200
These are optional ways to describe an
experience, rather than measures

648
00:46:53.200 --> 00:46:54.240
of ability.

649
00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:59.220
The important question is what costs
energy and what helps restore it.

650
00:47:00.060 --> 00:47:03.650
Rest, familiar interests or a
better-fitting environment may help

651
00:47:03.650 --> 00:47:04.420
recharge.

652
00:47:04.420 --> 00:47:08.750
Support needs to match the charge that is
actually available, not the

653
00:47:08.750 --> 00:47:10.920
amount we hoped would be there.

654
00:47:12.800 --> 00:47:16.640
Capacity can vary across a day and from
one day to another.

655
00:47:16.640 --> 00:47:22.890
Stress, sensory demands, sleep, social
situations and other events can

656
00:47:22.890 --> 00:47:25.160
change what feels manageable.

657
00:47:25.160 --> 00:47:28.920
The same request may be easy on one
occasion and too much on another.

658
00:47:28.920 --> 00:47:32.630
Think about what seems to use your child's
energy and what lightens the

659
00:47:32.630 --> 00:47:32.880
load.

660
00:47:32.880 --> 00:47:36.360
We don't need to interpret a change in
capacity as unwillingness.

661
00:47:36.360 --> 00:47:40.350
It gives us a reason to look again at the
demands and the support

662
00:47:40.350 --> 00:47:41.200
available.

663
00:47:43.360 --> 00:47:46.700
Masking can place another demand on
energy.

664
00:47:46.700 --> 00:47:49.720
We'll hear a first-person account now.

665
00:47:49.720 --> 00:47:51.900
Let the speaker describe their own
experience,

666
00:47:51.900 --> 00:47:55.620
and leave a little room afterwards to
reflect.

667
00:49:28.080 --> 00:49:30.080
Take a moment before we move on.

668
00:49:33.200 --> 00:49:36.080
The image places two moments beside one
another:

669
00:49:36.080 --> 00:49:40.540
managing at school, and arriving home with
very little energy left.

670
00:49:40.540 --> 00:49:43.920
Appearing fine may not show the effort
underneath.

671
00:49:43.920 --> 00:49:47.600
That doesn't tell us exactly what is
happening for every child.

672
00:49:47.600 --> 00:49:52.060
It reminds us to consider what a day
costs, including effort we may not

673
00:49:52.060 --> 00:49:52.840
see.

674
00:49:52.840 --> 00:49:56.770
Changes to the environment and
expectations can sometimes reduce that

675
00:49:56.770 --> 00:49:57.440
effort.

676
00:49:57.440 --> 00:50:00.640
We'll return to the after-school
experience in our next session.

677
00:50:02.160 --> 00:50:06.000
On page ten, consider where your child
seems to work hard to appear fine,

678
00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:09.160
and where they seem most comfortable being
themselves.

679
00:50:09.160 --> 00:50:12.960
What could adults change to help them feel
more supported?

680
00:50:12.960 --> 00:50:15.660
Start with private thinking.

681
00:50:15.660 --> 00:50:19.700
You do not need to prove masking is
occurring, or share a personal story.

682
00:50:19.700 --> 00:50:22.660
Keep your notes descriptive and your
questions open.

683
00:50:22.660 --> 00:50:26.700
If you wish, discuss one practical change
with your table.

684
00:50:26.700 --> 00:50:28.816
Pause here.

685
00:50:36.840 --> 00:50:40.000
Keep that possible change in mind.

686
00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:43.720
Recognising something now doesn't mean you
should have known it earlier.

687
00:50:43.720 --> 00:50:46.920
We can learn from new understanding
without blame.

688
00:50:46.920 --> 00:50:50.920
Making room for a person to be themselves
connects directly to the idea

689
00:50:50.920 --> 00:50:52.280
on our closing slide.

690
00:50:55.320 --> 00:50:58.760
Across these two sessions, we've been
building a picture of your child.

691
00:50:58.760 --> 00:51:03.150
How the world arrives through their
senses. How they notice detail,

692
00:51:03.150 --> 00:51:07.600
focus deeply and bring particular
strengths. How they experience

693
00:51:07.600 --> 00:51:11.760
internal signals, communicate and reach
towards other people.

694
00:51:11.760 --> 00:51:16.060
Managing those demands every day can take
a great deal of energy,

695
00:51:16.060 --> 00:51:18.400
especially when the surroundings don't
fit.

696
00:51:19.440 --> 00:51:24.330
Sensory load, transitions, communication
across different styles, and

697
00:51:24.330 --> 00:51:27.760
sometimes appearing fine can all add to
the cost.

698
00:51:27.760 --> 00:51:32.070
A child who knows who they are, and feels
valued for it, has more room

699
00:51:32.070 --> 00:51:35.740
for learning, connection and
self-understanding.

700
00:51:35.740 --> 00:51:37.580
Belonging matters.

701
00:51:37.580 --> 00:51:41.890
So does genuine choice about how they
communicate, where they feel

702
00:51:41.890 --> 00:51:43.740
comfortable and when they need a break.

703
00:51:43.740 --> 00:51:48.030
Knowing other Autistic people can offer
connection and a sense of

704
00:51:48.030 --> 00:51:49.040
shared experience.

705
00:51:50.160 --> 00:51:53.240
Think back to the strengths and joys you
wrote down this morning.

706
00:51:53.240 --> 00:51:57.100
Supporting a positive Autistic identity
can begin with listening,

707
00:51:57.100 --> 00:52:00.920
making room for those strengths, and
showing that this whole person is

708
00:52:00.920 --> 00:52:02.060
worth knowing.

709
00:52:02.060 --> 00:52:04.280
We'll pause here.

710
00:52:04.280 --> 00:52:06.220
Take your lunch break when you're ready.

711
00:52:06.220 --> 00:52:09.020
When we return, we'll consider what this
understanding can look like at

712
00:52:09.020 --> 00:52:10.240
home and in the community.

713
00:52:21.920 --> 00:52:25.950
Welcome back. This morning was about
understanding Autistic experience.

714
00:52:25.950 --> 00:52:27.360
Now we consider what that

715
00:52:27.360 --> 00:52:31.530
understanding can mean in everyday family
life. You know your family

716
00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:33.440
best. Take the ideas that

717
00:52:33.440 --> 00:52:37.520
fit and leave those that don't. We'll look
at distress and recovery,

718
00:52:37.520 --> 00:52:38.560
familiar routines,

719
00:52:38.560 --> 00:52:41.840
interests, outings and the needs of the
whole family.

720
00:52:41.840 --> 00:52:47.150
We are looking for a manageable next step,
with your child's preferences

721
00:52:47.150 --> 00:52:49.040
and capacity at the centre.

722
00:52:50.560 --> 00:52:53.400
What we can see and hear may be only the
tip of the iceberg.

723
00:52:53.400 --> 00:52:57.940
Underneath can sit thoughts, feelings,
values and needs,

724
00:52:57.940 --> 00:53:01.960
including belonging, predictability,
security and self-expression.

725
00:53:01.960 --> 00:53:05.100
Think privately of a hard moment.

726
00:53:05.100 --> 00:53:07.400
What had happened beforehand?

727
00:53:07.400 --> 00:53:12.080
Perhaps there was an unexpected change, a
noisy shop,

728
00:53:12.080 --> 00:53:15.680
or a request that was simply too big for
that day.

729
00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:18.960
We may not know the answer straight away.

730
00:53:19.880 --> 00:53:23.050
Behaviour can give us information, and
curiosity helps us look beyond

731
00:53:23.050 --> 00:53:23.860
the surface.

732
00:53:23.860 --> 00:53:27.340
This isn't about blaming a child or a
parent.

733
00:53:27.340 --> 00:53:30.680
It is about understanding the conditions
around a difficult moment.

734
00:53:33.280 --> 00:53:35.720
Overwhelm can turn inward or outward.

735
00:53:35.720 --> 00:53:38.760
A shutdown may look like becoming very
still, freezing,

736
00:53:38.760 --> 00:53:40.720
withdrawing or being less able to speak

737
00:53:40.720 --> 00:53:42.520
and explain what is needed.

738
00:53:42.520 --> 00:53:45.560
A meltdown may involve crying, running
away

739
00:53:45.560 --> 00:53:48.840
or a large outward expression of distress.

740
00:53:48.840 --> 00:53:52.040
These are involuntary responses to
overwhelm.

741
00:53:52.040 --> 00:53:54.240
Quietness can be easy to miss,

742
00:53:54.240 --> 00:53:56.600
especially when someone seems to be
managing.

743
00:53:56.600 --> 00:53:59.360
The response alone may not tell us the
full story.

744
00:53:59.360 --> 00:54:02.320
Keep the person's safety and need for
reduced demands

745
00:54:02.320 --> 00:54:06.080
in view, and allow some space around these
experiences.

746
00:54:06.080 --> 00:54:08.480
They can be hard for families to revisit.

747
00:54:10.360 --> 00:54:13.040
Sometimes overload builds over a long
period.

748
00:54:13.040 --> 00:54:16.120
Autistic burnout describes deep physical,
mental

749
00:54:16.120 --> 00:54:18.560
and emotional exhaustion, with a reduction

750
00:54:18.560 --> 00:54:20.920
in capacity that may last.

751
00:54:20.920 --> 00:54:23.040
School Can't describes being unable to
attend

752
00:54:23.040 --> 00:54:24.840
because school has become overwhelming

753
00:54:24.840 --> 00:54:28.760
through anxiety, fear or sensory load.

754
00:54:28.760 --> 00:54:31.400
These terms help us consider what someone
may have been

755
00:54:31.400 --> 00:54:34.880
carrying and what support or recovery they
need.

756
00:54:34.880 --> 00:54:37.280
They don't let us diagnose an individual
situation

757
00:54:37.280 --> 00:54:38.600
from a slide.

758
00:54:38.600 --> 00:54:42.240
If your family is living through this, it
can be very hard.

759
00:54:42.240 --> 00:54:44.640
A conversation with appropriate health and
school supports

760
00:54:44.640 --> 00:54:47.560
can help explore the individual picture.

761
00:54:47.560 --> 00:54:49.640
We'll look at working with school in
Session Five.

762
00:54:51.160 --> 00:54:54.570
In the moment, try reducing input: fewer
words, fewer questions and

763
00:54:54.570 --> 00:54:55.380
less noise.

764
00:54:55.380 --> 00:54:59.370
A quiet, safe space works best when you've
talked about it together

765
00:54:59.370 --> 00:55:01.500
beforehand, while everyone is calm.

766
00:55:01.500 --> 00:55:04.840
Keep your approach matched to what you
know helps your child.

767
00:55:04.840 --> 00:55:08.600
A distressed person may have very little
capacity for explanation or

768
00:55:08.600 --> 00:55:09.780
problem-solving.

769
00:55:09.780 --> 00:55:13.120
The conversation about what happened can
wait until they are ready.

770
00:55:13.120 --> 00:55:17.530
On page twelve, there's room to gather
early signs, what helps in the

771
00:55:17.530 --> 00:55:19.780
moment and what helps recovery.

772
00:55:19.900 --> 00:55:21.660
You can add to that picture over time.

773
00:55:21.660 --> 00:55:25.410
These are options to consider, rather than
a script every family has to

774
00:55:25.410 --> 00:55:26.080
follow.

775
00:55:28.120 --> 00:55:31.690
Co-regulation means a trusted adult
helping a child settle through a

776
00:55:31.690 --> 00:55:33.720
steady, responsive presence.

777
00:55:33.720 --> 00:55:36.760
Sometimes that involves very few words.

778
00:55:36.760 --> 00:55:41.480
Self-regulation is what a person does to
manage their own energy and

779
00:55:41.480 --> 00:55:41.920
feelings,

780
00:55:41.920 --> 00:55:44.080
and it develops with support over time.

781
00:55:44.080 --> 00:55:47.960
Nobody manages to be calm every time.

782
00:55:47.960 --> 00:55:51.880
If you're exhausted or upset yourself,
this can be hard.

783
00:55:51.880 --> 00:55:55.360
The point is to notice the support both of
you need, and to make room

784
00:55:55.360 --> 00:55:56.760
for it where possible.

785
00:55:57.440 --> 00:56:00.240
We'll return to your own battery later in
this session.

786
00:56:02.440 --> 00:56:05.420
Some families recognise an after-school
crash.

787
00:56:05.420 --> 00:56:09.180
A child may appear to manage throughout
school, then have very little

788
00:56:09.180 --> 00:56:10.240
left when they get home.

789
00:56:10.240 --> 00:56:14.820
What school sees and what you see can both
be real parts of the day.

790
00:56:14.820 --> 00:56:20.120
A softer landing might mean food, quiet,
movement or time with a screen

791
00:56:20.120 --> 00:56:23.720
or favourite activity, guided by what
helps your child recover.

792
00:56:23.720 --> 00:56:28.020
Questions about the day, and homework, can
wait.

793
00:56:28.020 --> 00:56:32.020
A familiar after-school routine can make
that recovery predictable.

794
00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:36.740
Your child may talk later, perhaps at
bedtime, or may not want to talk

795
00:56:36.740 --> 00:56:37.880
about school.

796
00:56:37.880 --> 00:56:39.720
Notice what helps them settle.

797
00:56:39.720 --> 00:56:43.460
What happens at home can give school
useful information about the

798
00:56:43.460 --> 00:56:44.520
overall load.

799
00:56:46.320 --> 00:56:50.040
Predictability can reduce uncertainty, and
it doesn't require turning

800
00:56:50.040 --> 00:56:51.840
home into a classroom.

801
00:56:51.840 --> 00:56:55.680
Choose one or two routines that would help
your family most.

802
00:56:55.680 --> 00:57:00.880
Perhaps mornings, after school or bedtime
need a clearer sequence.

803
00:57:00.880 --> 00:57:05.520
A schedule on the fridge or a now-and-next
card may help where it makes

804
00:57:05.520 --> 00:57:06.480
information easier

805
00:57:06.480 --> 00:57:07.480
to understand.

806
00:57:07.480 --> 00:57:12.040
If you give a five-minute warning, try to
make it mean five minutes.

807
00:57:12.040 --> 00:57:16.390
When plans change, name the change, show
it if helpful and give as much

808
00:57:16.390 --> 00:57:17.280
warning as you

809
00:57:17.280 --> 00:57:18.920
can.

810
00:57:18.920 --> 00:57:21.160
The routine should serve your family.

811
00:57:21.160 --> 00:57:24.390
If it creates more work and stress than it
solves, it needs another

812
00:57:24.390 --> 00:57:24.800
look.

813
00:57:27.320 --> 00:57:31.280
Interests can be an anchor for regulation,
connection and joy.

814
00:57:31.280 --> 00:57:35.360
Time with a familiar interest may help
restore energy.

815
00:57:35.360 --> 00:57:38.800
It can also offer a way into your child's
world:

816
00:57:38.800 --> 00:57:41.040
listening while they explain,

817
00:57:41.040 --> 00:57:44.520
watching what they make or joining in when
invited.

818
00:57:44.520 --> 00:57:48.560
An interest is part of their experience
and identity.

819
00:57:48.560 --> 00:57:51.080
Taking it away as a consequence can also
take away

820
00:57:51.080 --> 00:57:52.400
a source of regulation.

821
00:57:52.400 --> 00:57:55.720
If that approach has been familiar at
home,

822
00:57:55.720 --> 00:57:58.000
you can consider the function the interest
serves

823
00:57:58.000 --> 00:58:00.680
and what another response might look like.

824
00:58:00.680 --> 00:58:03.240
Stay curious about what it gives your
child.

825
00:58:05.360 --> 00:58:07.680
Turn to Planning an outing on page twelve.

826
00:58:07.680 --> 00:58:10.080
Choose something familiar or coming up:

827
00:58:10.080 --> 00:58:12.600
a party, a shopping trip or a swimming
lesson.

828
00:58:12.600 --> 00:58:14.880
Think about before, during and after.

829
00:58:14.880 --> 00:58:17.480
Beforehand, what can you preview?

830
00:58:17.480 --> 00:58:18.940
Where are we going,

831
00:58:18.940 --> 00:58:20.020
what will happen,

832
00:58:20.020 --> 00:58:21.460
how long might it be,

833
00:58:21.460 --> 00:58:23.000
and what if it is too much?

834
00:58:23.000 --> 00:58:27.360
During the outing, how will you notice
energy and use an exit plan?

835
00:58:27.360 --> 00:58:30.420
Afterwards, what recovery time might be
needed?

836
00:58:30.420 --> 00:58:33.340
Share one idea at your table if you wish,

837
00:58:33.340 --> 00:58:36.120
and add any useful ideas to your notes.

838
00:58:36.120 --> 00:58:39.480
If outings aren't workable for your family
right now,

839
00:58:39.480 --> 00:58:42.360
you can choose a smaller situation or
simply listen.

840
00:58:42.360 --> 00:58:45.140
Pause here for the activity.

841
00:58:54.200 --> 00:58:57.680
Bring your attention back to the plan.

842
00:58:57.680 --> 00:59:01.300
Adjusting an outing, leaving early or
deciding against it can all be

843
00:59:01.300 --> 00:59:02.760
sensible choices.

844
00:59:02.760 --> 00:59:07.360
The useful question is whether the plan
fits your child and family.

845
00:59:07.360 --> 00:59:11.320
Keep the exit and recovery alongside the
practical details,

846
00:59:11.320 --> 00:59:15.400
and check the plan with your child in a
way they can access.

847
00:59:16.960 --> 00:59:20.160
Family life includes everyone's needs.

848
00:59:20.160 --> 00:59:21.580
Where there are siblings,

849
00:59:21.580 --> 00:59:25.520
small regular moments of individual
attention can matter.

850
00:59:25.520 --> 00:59:28.660
Fairness may mean each child getting what
they need,

851
00:59:28.660 --> 00:59:30.800
even when that looks different.

852
00:59:30.800 --> 00:59:34.400
Words that fit their age can help siblings
understand

853
00:59:34.400 --> 00:59:36.160
what is happening.

854
00:59:36.160 --> 00:59:39.100
Families are arranged in many different
ways.

855
00:59:39.100 --> 00:59:41.240
There isn't a formula for getting the
balance right,

856
00:59:41.240 --> 00:59:44.360
and you don't need to share private family
difficulties here.

857
00:59:44.360 --> 00:59:48.020
Look for something sustainable in your own
circumstances.

858
00:59:48.020 --> 00:59:51.000
The What works in our home page has space
for ideas

859
00:59:51.000 --> 00:59:52.520
if this topic is useful to you.

860
00:59:54.360 --> 00:59:58.200
Your own battery counts too. Helping
another person settle is harder

861
00:59:58.200 --> 00:59:58.760
when you are running

862
00:59:58.760 --> 01:00:04.630
on empty. On page thirteen, take a moment
with My own battery. What small

863
01:00:04.630 --> 01:00:06.760
thing helps you recharge?

864
01:00:06.760 --> 01:00:10.090
Who, if anyone, could help make this week
a little lighter? It might be a

865
01:00:10.090 --> 01:00:10.600
short walk, a

866
01:00:10.600 --> 01:00:14.550
few quiet minutes, a conversation or
practical help with a task. If

867
01:00:14.550 --> 01:00:15.720
support is scarce, that

868
01:00:15.720 --> 01:00:20.450
is a real constraint, not something you
have done wrong. Keep the idea

869
01:00:20.450 --> 01:00:21.200
realistic. Pause

870
01:00:21.200 --> 01:00:22.640
here to think or write.

871
01:00:31.120 --> 01:00:33.400
Keep whatever feels possible.

872
01:00:33.400 --> 01:00:36.600
Looking after your capacity is part of
family planning.

873
01:00:36.600 --> 01:00:39.480
Sometimes the useful step is reducing one
demand

874
01:00:39.480 --> 01:00:41.800
or asking for specific help.

875
01:00:41.800 --> 01:00:44.600
It says nothing about how much you love
your child,

876
01:00:44.600 --> 01:00:47.560
and it doesn't have to become another task
to succeed at.

877
01:00:49.920 --> 01:00:53.280
Families know a lot. Let's make room for
that knowledge.

878
01:00:53.280 --> 01:00:56.600
On page thirteen, choose one thing that
works in your home.

879
01:00:56.600 --> 01:01:00.560
It could relate to mornings, after school,
outings,

880
01:01:00.560 --> 01:01:03.560
bedtime, siblings or your own energy.

881
01:01:03.560 --> 01:01:06.320
At your table, each person can offer one
idea,

882
01:01:06.320 --> 01:01:08.400
with the option to pass.

883
01:01:08.400 --> 01:01:10.360
Listen for something that might fit your
family

884
01:01:10.360 --> 01:01:12.160
and write it down.

885
01:01:12.160 --> 01:01:15.800
We don't need to solve one family's whole
situation.

886
01:01:15.800 --> 01:01:19.000
If you're watching alone, note what
already works

887
01:01:19.000 --> 01:01:21.520
and one idea you might explore.

888
01:01:21.520 --> 01:01:23.000
Pause now for the sharing.

889
01:01:31.320 --> 01:01:35.560
Choose one idea to keep, and leave room to
adapt it.

890
01:01:35.560 --> 01:01:40.520
What works in another home may need to
look different in yours.

891
01:01:40.520 --> 01:01:44.200
As we move into partnership with school,
keep one observation from home

892
01:01:44.200 --> 01:01:45.320
in mind.

893
01:01:45.320 --> 01:01:48.360
It may be something school needs to
understand about your child's day.

894
01:01:54.960 --> 01:01:57.000
Now we turn to partnership with school.

895
01:01:57.000 --> 01:01:59.560
Keep that home observation in mind.

896
01:01:59.560 --> 01:02:01.160
Your child, your family and the school

897
01:02:01.160 --> 01:02:03.720
may each hold different parts of the
picture.

898
01:02:03.720 --> 01:02:07.520
Bringing those together can help shape
useful support.

899
01:02:07.520 --> 01:02:09.960
People arrive with very different school
experiences.

900
01:02:09.960 --> 01:02:11.520
Some relationships feel helpful,

901
01:02:11.520 --> 01:02:13.640
and others have been difficult.

902
01:02:13.640 --> 01:02:16.120
You can take part without sharing a
personal account.

903
01:02:16.120 --> 01:02:18.640
Please keep staff, schools and children

904
01:02:18.640 --> 01:02:20.820
unidentifiable in group discussion.

905
01:02:22.640 --> 01:02:26.770
Look at the overlapping partnerships:
family, school staff and support

906
01:02:26.770 --> 01:02:29.280
staff, with your child at the centre.

907
01:02:29.280 --> 01:02:33.640
Each person brings different knowledge and
a different relationship.

908
01:02:33.640 --> 01:02:37.920
Who else belongs in that picture for your
family: perhaps a grandparent,

909
01:02:37.920 --> 01:02:40.640
therapist or out-of-school-hours carer?

910
01:02:40.640 --> 01:02:43.640
What is the shared purpose of these
partnerships?

911
01:02:43.640 --> 01:02:47.640
How can your child's own communication
help shape it?

912
01:02:47.640 --> 01:02:52.640
Take a moment to reflect or discuss, and
pause if you'd like more time.

913
01:03:00.720 --> 01:03:03.920
Keep your child at the centre as you
consider the answer.

914
01:03:03.920 --> 01:03:06.720
No single person holds the whole picture.

915
01:03:06.720 --> 01:03:10.640
A useful partnership helps people
understand each other's knowledge

916
01:03:10.640 --> 01:03:13.600
and agree on support that makes a
difference to the child.

917
01:03:15.480 --> 01:03:19.110
The Family-School Partnerships Framework
recognises parents and

918
01:03:19.110 --> 01:03:22.480
families as children's first and
continuing educators.

919
01:03:22.480 --> 01:03:26.480
Learning happens across settings, and a
family's diversity and strengths

920
01:03:26.480 --> 01:03:28.040
matter to that learning.

921
01:03:28.040 --> 01:03:32.150
The other principles include community
involvement, mutual trust and

922
01:03:32.150 --> 01:03:33.480
shared responsibility,

923
01:03:33.480 --> 01:03:36.280
supported by committed and collaborative
leadership.

924
01:03:36.280 --> 01:03:40.850
In an everyday conversation, look for
respectful listening, clear

925
01:03:40.850 --> 01:03:44.920
information, your child's views and agreed
next steps.

926
01:03:46.480 --> 01:03:48.710
Family knowledge and school knowledge are
both needed from the

927
01:03:48.710 --> 01:03:49.480
beginning.

928
01:03:49.480 --> 01:03:52.620
Respectful listening is possible even when
people see a situation

929
01:03:52.620 --> 01:03:53.480
differently.

930
01:03:56.600 --> 01:03:58.920
What makes collaboration work?

931
01:03:58.920 --> 01:04:01.960
Think of a situation where people have
worked well together,

932
01:04:01.960 --> 01:04:04.960
inside or outside school.

933
01:04:04.960 --> 01:04:08.160
What helped them listen and make a
decision?

934
01:04:08.160 --> 01:04:11.440
Share a brief idea or reflect privately.

935
01:04:11.440 --> 01:04:13.360
Pause here if you would like time.

936
01:04:21.480 --> 01:04:26.470
The slide brings together shared goals and
values, mutual trust,

937
01:04:26.470 --> 01:04:28.400
consultation, curiosity

938
01:04:28.400 --> 01:04:31.840
and attention to different perspectives.

939
01:04:31.840 --> 01:04:34.280
Your child's voice belongs throughout.

940
01:04:34.280 --> 01:04:38.020
The phrase assume good intent may feel
difficult if trust has been

941
01:04:38.020 --> 01:04:39.000
damaged. Trust

942
01:04:39.000 --> 01:04:43.400
needs action from both sides, and it can
take time to rebuild.

943
01:04:43.400 --> 01:04:47.940
A possible opening is: how could we work
together so school goes well

944
01:04:47.940 --> 01:04:49.120
for my child?

945
01:04:49.120 --> 01:04:52.860
That gives the conversation a purpose
while leaving room to discuss

946
01:04:52.860 --> 01:04:54.080
what needs to change.

947
01:04:56.160 --> 01:05:00.160
There are also responsibilities that sit
behind these conversations.

948
01:05:00.160 --> 01:05:04.210
The Disability Discrimination Act protects
people from discrimination

949
01:05:04.210 --> 01:05:05.880
because of disability,

950
01:05:05.880 --> 01:05:08.080
including in education.

951
01:05:08.080 --> 01:05:11.570
The Disability Standards for Education
explain obligations to support

952
01:05:11.570 --> 01:05:12.800
access and participation

953
01:05:12.800 --> 01:05:17.280
on the same basis as students without
disability.

954
01:05:17.280 --> 01:05:21.380
Schools must consult the student, or an
associate such as a parent or

955
01:05:21.380 --> 01:05:22.680
carer, when considering

956
01:05:22.680 --> 01:05:25.440
reasonable adjustments.

957
01:05:25.440 --> 01:05:28.760
Useful questions are: how will we be
consulted?

958
01:05:28.760 --> 01:05:30.720
What adjustments are in place?

959
01:05:30.720 --> 01:05:34.360
How and when will they be reviewed?

960
01:05:34.360 --> 01:05:38.710
This is general information, rather than
advice about an individual

961
01:05:38.710 --> 01:05:40.080
legal situation.

962
01:05:40.080 --> 01:05:43.430
The Department of Education's
plain-language resources explain these

963
01:05:43.430 --> 01:05:44.800
rights and responsibilities

964
01:05:44.800 --> 01:05:45.800
for families.

965
01:05:45.800 --> 01:05:49.200
They are a useful place to return to after
the workshop.

966
01:05:51.680 --> 01:05:56.310
Power can sit in the way a meeting is
arranged, the language used and

967
01:05:56.310 --> 01:05:57.360
who can make decisions.

968
01:05:57.360 --> 01:06:02.800
Familiarity with school systems, cultural
and historical experiences of

969
01:06:02.800 --> 01:06:03.840
education,

970
01:06:03.840 --> 01:06:07.520
language and financial circumstances can
also shape how a conversation

971
01:06:07.520 --> 01:06:07.920
feels.

972
01:06:07.920 --> 01:06:13.360
Who sets the agenda? Whose knowledge gets
heard? Is your child included

973
01:06:13.360 --> 01:06:14.640
in a way that works for them?

974
01:06:14.640 --> 01:06:17.680
What might need to change about the
process?

975
01:06:18.800 --> 01:06:22.000
Naming these features can help us plan a
more balanced conversation.

976
01:06:22.000 --> 01:06:26.000
We can examine the meeting itself without
turning the discussion into a

977
01:06:26.000 --> 01:06:28.640
judgement about an identifiable person.

978
01:06:31.000 --> 01:06:36.040
Use the Power dynamics activity on page
fifteen, or three sticky notes.

979
01:06:36.040 --> 01:06:39.520
First, describe an imbalance from a home
perspective.

980
01:06:39.520 --> 01:06:42.440
On the second, consider one from a school
perspective.

981
01:06:42.440 --> 01:06:46.840
On the third, suggest how the imbalance
could be addressed.

982
01:06:46.840 --> 01:06:50.080
Thinking about another perspective doesn't
mean excusing poor practice.

983
01:06:50.080 --> 01:06:54.040
It helps us identify what could change in
a conversation.

984
01:06:54.040 --> 01:06:58.360
You can share at your table, add anonymous
notes under Home, School

985
01:06:58.360 --> 01:07:01.640
and Solutions, or keep your notes private.

986
01:07:01.640 --> 01:07:02.920
Pause now for the activity.

987
01:07:10.960 --> 01:07:14.280
Look again at the solutions. Could the
agenda be shared earlier?

988
01:07:14.280 --> 01:07:16.120
Could language be clearer,

989
01:07:16.120 --> 01:07:18.240
a support person be included,

990
01:07:18.240 --> 01:07:19.800
or another way of contributing

991
01:07:19.800 --> 01:07:21.320
be available?

992
01:07:21.320 --> 01:07:25.000
Choose one idea that would make
participation more workable.

993
01:07:25.000 --> 01:07:27.240
Keep it for the conversation plan later in
this session.

994
01:07:28.800 --> 01:07:30.160
We're going to hear from Adon.

995
01:07:30.160 --> 01:07:34.740
As you watch, pay attention to what
decisions were made and who made

996
01:07:34.740 --> 01:07:35.080
them.

997
01:07:35.080 --> 01:07:40.140
Notice the effect of being included, or
not included, in decisions about

998
01:07:40.140 --> 01:07:41.520
your own experience.

999
01:09:36.600 --> 01:09:40.120
What impact did those decisions have on
Adon?

1000
01:09:40.120 --> 01:09:43.840
Make a note on page fifteen, or discuss
the question briefly.

1001
01:09:43.840 --> 01:09:49.320
Then consider how your child's preferences
are asked for and heard.

1002
01:09:49.320 --> 01:09:50.760
Pause here if you'd like more time.

1003
01:09:58.880 --> 01:10:02.080
Children can have a say through the ways
they communicate:

1004
01:10:02.080 --> 01:10:07.040
words, drawings, typing, visual choices,
gesture or other responses.

1005
01:10:07.040 --> 01:10:11.010
We can ask what helps, what is hard and
what they want a teacher to

1006
01:10:11.010 --> 01:10:11.680
know.

1007
01:10:11.680 --> 01:10:14.950
Their own perspective belongs in the
conversation, including when it

1008
01:10:14.950 --> 01:10:17.120
differs from what adults expected.

1009
01:10:19.040 --> 01:10:22.000
Turn to What works on page sixteen.

1010
01:10:22.000 --> 01:10:24.040
Think of a time you worked well with
someone at school.

1011
01:10:24.040 --> 01:10:25.560
What made it work?

1012
01:10:25.560 --> 01:10:28.760
What did they do, and what did you do?

1013
01:10:28.760 --> 01:10:30.880
If a school example is difficult to find,

1014
01:10:30.880 --> 01:10:33.040
choose another helpful relationship

1015
01:10:33.040 --> 01:10:35.040
or listen to ideas from others.

1016
01:10:35.040 --> 01:10:37.560
Talk at your table without naming staff or
schools,

1017
01:10:37.560 --> 01:10:40.280
and note anything worth carrying forward.

1018
01:10:40.280 --> 01:10:41.760
Pause for that reflection.

1019
01:10:50.880 --> 01:10:54.520
The practical moves on the slide are
useful starting points:

1020
01:10:54.520 --> 01:10:59.680
prepare, share information, ask questions
and seek clarification.

1021
01:10:59.680 --> 01:11:03.380
You can ask for an agenda beforehand,
arrange to bring a support person

1022
01:11:03.380 --> 01:11:05.320
and bring your own notes.

1023
01:11:05.320 --> 01:11:09.770
Ask for a record of agreed actions, then
follow up in writing so

1024
01:11:09.770 --> 01:11:12.280
everyone can check what was understood.

1025
01:11:12.280 --> 01:11:16.730
If trust is strained, a clear purpose and
a small agreed step may be a

1026
01:11:16.730 --> 01:11:17.480
beginning.

1027
01:11:19.480 --> 01:11:23.930
Consultation is more useful when there is
a clear shared purpose, open

1028
01:11:23.930 --> 01:11:26.480
questions and more than one way to
contribute.

1029
01:11:26.480 --> 01:11:30.480
Your knowledge and your child's knowledge
deserve attention.

1030
01:11:30.480 --> 01:11:35.000
What was heard needs to be recorded
accurately, followed by an agreed

1031
01:11:35.000 --> 01:11:37.480
action, responsibility and follow-up.

1032
01:11:37.480 --> 01:11:42.480
The checklist on page sixteen can help
before and after a meeting.

1033
01:11:42.480 --> 01:11:45.480
Ask, listen and follow up.

1034
01:11:45.480 --> 01:11:48.950
Before leaving, check who will do what and
when you will hear how it is

1035
01:11:48.950 --> 01:11:49.480
going.

1036
01:11:49.480 --> 01:11:53.920
A conversation becomes more useful when
everyone understands the next

1037
01:11:53.920 --> 01:11:54.480
step.

1038
01:11:56.680 --> 01:12:00.410
The Working Together Communication Tool,
on the left, helps you agree how

1039
01:12:00.410 --> 01:12:01.760
home and school will communicate.

1040
01:12:01.760 --> 01:12:06.560
You can choose a method, such as email,
phone or a communication book,

1041
01:12:06.560 --> 01:12:10.960
then record how often, what you'll
communicate about, and the next dates

1042
01:12:10.960 --> 01:12:11.740
or steps.

1043
01:12:11.740 --> 01:12:15.760
The Partnership Planning Template, on the
right, asks what has worked,

1044
01:12:15.760 --> 01:12:18.220
what has been challenging and what could
help.

1045
01:12:18.220 --> 01:12:21.980
You can use those prompts before a meeting
or complete them together.

1046
01:12:21.980 --> 01:12:24.780
A tool gives the conversation a place to
start.

1047
01:12:24.900 --> 01:12:27.740
Keep listening and your child's own
communication alongside it.

1048
01:12:29.920 --> 01:12:34.080
The N C C D Meetings Tool Kit is a free
resource for families and schools

1049
01:12:34.080 --> 01:12:35.520
preparing for meetings

1050
01:12:35.520 --> 01:12:37.320
about adjustments.

1051
01:12:37.320 --> 01:12:40.520
It covers before, during and after a
meeting.

1052
01:12:40.520 --> 01:12:43.920
The link is in the resources at the back
of your workbook.

1053
01:12:43.920 --> 01:12:47.120
You can use it to think about what
information to bring and what to

1054
01:12:47.120 --> 01:12:48.120
clarify.

1055
01:12:48.120 --> 01:12:52.720
Your school may have its own process, so
ask how consultation and

1056
01:12:52.720 --> 01:12:54.920
follow-up will work in your setting.

1057
01:12:56.600 --> 01:12:59.240
Help is available beyond this workshop.

1058
01:12:59.240 --> 01:13:03.080
Autism Connect offers autism information
and support.

1059
01:13:03.080 --> 01:13:06.520
Disability Gateway helps people find
disability information

1060
01:13:06.520 --> 01:13:08.240
and services. Carer

1061
01:13:08.240 --> 01:13:10.960
Gateway provides support for carers.

1062
01:13:10.960 --> 01:13:14.120
Their contact details are at the back of
your workbook.

1063
01:13:14.120 --> 01:13:18.640
Lifeline's crisis support number is
thirteen, eleven, fourteen.

1064
01:13:18.640 --> 01:13:21.760
Kids Helpline provides counselling for
children

1065
01:13:21.760 --> 01:13:28.960
and young people aged five to twenty-five
on eighteen hundred, fifty-five, eighteen
hundred.

1066
01:13:28.960 --> 01:13:31.920
Its website also has information for
parents and carers,

1067
01:13:31.920 --> 01:13:37.800
and points parents towards Parentline for
parenting counselling.

1068
01:13:37.800 --> 01:13:40.840
Take a moment to note the support that is
relevant to you.

1069
01:13:40.840 --> 01:13:43.840
You don't have to work everything out by
yourself.

1070
01:13:48.960 --> 01:13:53.360
Turn to page seventeen and choose one
purposeful conversation with school.

1071
01:13:53.360 --> 01:13:55.560
What needs to be understood?

1072
01:13:55.560 --> 01:13:58.360
What question could open the conversation?

1073
01:13:58.360 --> 01:14:01.360
How will your child's views be included?

1074
01:14:01.360 --> 01:14:04.960
What could be decided together, and how
will you follow up?

1075
01:14:04.960 --> 01:14:06.960
Use short notes.

1076
01:14:06.960 --> 01:14:09.700
Bring in one idea from the power activity
that could make the

1077
01:14:09.700 --> 01:14:10.960
conversation more balanced.

1078
01:14:10.960 --> 01:14:14.960
There is no need to share a sensitive
situation.

1079
01:14:14.960 --> 01:14:16.960
Pause here and give yourself time to
prepare.

1080
01:14:25.120 --> 01:14:27.840
Circle the question you want to open with.

1081
01:14:27.840 --> 01:14:29.680
Keep these notes for Session Six.

1082
01:14:29.680 --> 01:14:33.600
A small, specific next step is enough:

1083
01:14:33.600 --> 01:14:35.360
one thing that needs to be understood,

1084
01:14:35.360 --> 01:14:37.520
and a way to hear from your child as part
of it.

1085
01:14:45.440 --> 01:14:49.200
We've reached Full Circle. Look back at
page four, at the child you

1086
01:14:49.200 --> 01:14:50.640
described and the one

1087
01:14:50.640 --> 01:14:56.400
thing you wanted to know. What feels
clearer now? What question remains?

1088
01:14:56.400 --> 01:14:57.520
Take a moment privately,

1089
01:14:57.520 --> 01:15:00.800
or share a brief reflection if you wish.
Then look again at the

1090
01:15:00.800 --> 01:15:02.800
strengths and joys you wrote down.

1091
01:15:02.800 --> 01:15:06.800
Pause here before we choose what to carry
forward.

1092
01:15:14.920 --> 01:15:17.360
Keep that whole person in view.

1093
01:15:17.360 --> 01:15:21.010
We're going to finish with a few small
actions that connect home,

1094
01:15:21.010 --> 01:15:23.360
school and positive identity.

1095
01:15:23.360 --> 01:15:26.270
You don't need a long list, and you don't
need to have answered every

1096
01:15:26.270 --> 01:15:27.000
question today.

1097
01:15:29.600 --> 01:15:34.470
Belonging, capacity and joy give us three
connected questions to return

1098
01:15:34.470 --> 01:15:35.200
to.

1099
01:15:35.200 --> 01:15:39.360
Can your child be recognised, included and
able to participate as

1100
01:15:39.360 --> 01:15:40.800
themselves?

1101
01:15:40.800 --> 01:15:42.800
What does the day cost,

1102
01:15:42.800 --> 01:15:45.400
and what protects or restores energy?

1103
01:15:45.400 --> 01:15:49.600
Where are the interests, connections and
moments of authentic enjoyment?

1104
01:15:49.600 --> 01:15:53.600
When you try a change at home or school,
look across all three.

1105
01:15:53.600 --> 01:15:57.000
Getting through a task is only part of the
picture.

1106
01:15:57.000 --> 01:16:00.400
The effect on energy and belonging matters
too.

1107
01:16:00.400 --> 01:16:04.520
Your child's own response will help you
judge whether a change is

1108
01:16:04.520 --> 01:16:05.400
useful.

1109
01:16:07.080 --> 01:16:12.800
Turn to Three small things on page
eighteen. First, one thing to try at home

1110
01:16:12.800 --> 01:16:13.320
this week.

1111
01:16:13.320 --> 01:16:18.020
Keep it small enough to begin. Second, one
thing you would like school

1112
01:16:18.020 --> 01:16:18.280
to know,

1113
01:16:18.280 --> 01:16:22.600
understand or support, and the sentence
you will open with.

1114
01:16:22.600 --> 01:16:29.160
Your notes from page seventeen can help.
Third, one strength you see in your

1115
01:16:29.160 --> 01:16:30.200
child, and a plan to

1116
01:16:30.200 --> 01:16:33.940
tell them this week. Look back at page
four if you need a starting

1117
01:16:33.940 --> 01:16:36.680
point. If this is a difficult

1118
01:16:36.680 --> 01:16:41.100
time and one strength is all you can
manage today, that is enough. Pause

1119
01:16:41.100 --> 01:16:43.800
the recording while you write.

1120
01:16:52.080 --> 01:16:53.920
Read your notes once more.

1121
01:16:53.920 --> 01:16:55.960
Are they manageable in your real week?

1122
01:16:55.960 --> 01:16:58.520
You can make a step smaller,

1123
01:16:58.520 --> 01:17:01.480
leave something for later or ask for help.

1124
01:17:01.480 --> 01:17:04.240
Keep the page somewhere you'll see it
again.

1125
01:17:04.240 --> 01:17:06.920
The plan can change as you learn what
helps.

1126
01:17:09.400 --> 01:17:13.860
Connection can continue through other
families, local or online parent

1127
01:17:13.860 --> 01:17:16.520
groups, and opportunities to learn
together.

1128
01:17:16.520 --> 01:17:21.270
If you're with others today, you can
exchange contact details if

1129
01:17:21.270 --> 01:17:22.800
everyone wishes.

1130
01:17:22.800 --> 01:17:25.040
Choose what fits you.

1131
01:17:25.040 --> 01:17:29.380
The supplied materials still list webinar
titles and dates as to be

1132
01:17:29.380 --> 01:17:33.380
confirmed, and the Community of Practice
link as to be added.

1133
01:17:33.380 --> 01:17:37.860
We can't give confirmed access details for
those yet.

1134
01:17:39.120 --> 01:17:42.290
Check current arrangements with the
workshop team or Positive

1135
01:17:42.290 --> 01:17:42.640
Partnerships.

1136
01:17:42.640 --> 01:17:47.440
Asking for support is part of parenting,
and there is room to keep

1137
01:17:47.440 --> 01:17:49.440
learning after today.

1138
01:17:52.480 --> 01:17:56.540
The Positive Partnerships website has
resources for families on many of

1139
01:17:56.540 --> 01:17:58.280
the topics we've explored.

1140
01:17:58.280 --> 01:18:02.080
The address is positive partnerships dot
com dot A U.

1141
01:18:02.080 --> 01:18:06.480
The slide also shows the organisation's
social media channels.

1142
01:18:06.480 --> 01:18:09.480
Pause if you would like to note or
photograph those details.

1143
01:18:09.480 --> 01:18:12.480
Your workbook has a resource list you can
return to later.

1144
01:18:16.160 --> 01:18:19.760
Feedback helps the team understand what
was useful and what could be

1145
01:18:19.760 --> 01:18:20.640
clearer.

1146
01:18:20.640 --> 01:18:25.610
This source slide still has a placeholder
for the feedback link and Q R

1147
01:18:25.610 --> 01:18:25.960
code,

1148
01:18:25.960 --> 01:18:29.040
so there is no active form to open here.

1149
01:18:29.040 --> 01:18:32.600
For now, you can write down one useful
idea, one remaining question

1150
01:18:32.600 --> 01:18:35.480
and anything that would make participation
easier.

1151
01:18:35.480 --> 01:18:38.880
Keep those notes to share through the
confirmed feedback arrangements

1152
01:18:38.880 --> 01:18:40.840
when they are available.

1153
01:18:40.840 --> 01:18:43.240
Pause here if you would like time.

1154
01:18:51.640 --> 01:18:53.640
Keep your feedback with your workbook.

1155
01:18:53.640 --> 01:18:57.380
Your experience can help shape how the
workshop is delivered for other

1156
01:18:57.380 --> 01:18:58.640
families.

1157
01:19:00.360 --> 01:19:03.320
Thank you for making time for this
learning and for the care you bring

1158
01:19:03.320 --> 01:19:04.840
to understanding your child.

1159
01:19:04.840 --> 01:19:08.120
Keep your workbook and the small steps
you've chosen.

1160
01:19:08.120 --> 01:19:13.400
Stay curious, keep your child at the
centre, and include the people who

1161
01:19:13.400 --> 01:19:13.960
know them well.

1162
01:19:13.960 --> 01:19:17.800
Try one manageable change, notice what
happens and adjust as you go.

1163
01:19:17.800 --> 01:19:21.800
There is room for a good day, a difficult
day and a fresh question.

1164
01:19:21.800 --> 01:19:25.580
The child you brought to mind at the
beginning remains the person we

1165
01:19:25.580 --> 01:19:26.440
come back to:

1166
01:19:26.440 --> 01:19:29.960
someone with strengths, interests and a
life worth understanding.
